<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<!--
  Entitlements for the macOS SCREENSHOT / UI-TEST build. Never shipped: the Mac
  App Store artifact uses BirdoVPN-macOS.entitlements.

  Exactly one key, and the reason is worth keeping.

  Sign-in cannot work without it. KeychainService writes with
  kSecAttrAccessGroup and kSecUseDataProtectionKeychain, and the
  data-protection keychain refuses an app that has no access group —
  "Could not securely store your session. Please try again."

  But carrying it under an AD-HOC signature made the app unlaunchable:

      Could not launch "BirdoVPN". Runningboard has returned error 5.
      Launchd job spawn failed.

  Measured, one key at a time, under ad-hoc signing:

      (no entitlements)                    app LAUNCHES, sign-in fails
      application-identifier + keychain    app will NOT launch
      keychain-access-groups alone         app will NOT launch

  The cause is the SIGNATURE, not the entitlement: an ad-hoc signature carries
  no team, so launchd cannot validate a team-prefixed entitlement and refuses
  the spawn. The build is therefore signed with a real Apple DEVELOPMENT
  certificate (team KPUFGR98A5), which has one. That also gets the runner
  attached: Xcode injects get-task-allow=true into development-signed Debug
  builds, and XCUITest cannot attach without it.

  No provisioning profile is used, deliberately. keychain-access-groups is not
  a restricted entitlement on macOS — it is validated against the signing
  identity's team, not authorised by a profile. Avoiding one matters because
  macOS DEVELOPMENT profiles must name registered devices
  ("ENTITY_ERROR.RELATIONSHIP.REQUIRED: The relationship 'devices' is
  required"), and an ephemeral CI runner is not one.

  The team prefix is written out rather than using $(AppIdentifierPrefix),
  which resolves from a provisioning profile this build does not have.

  Deliberately ABSENT:
    * com.apple.security.app-sandbox — a sandboxed build gets a different
      container and would not see the session it just wrote.
    * com.apple.developer.networking.networkextension — genuinely restricted,
      so it DOES need a profile. The packet tunnel cannot load here, which is
      why this job can never verify the tunnel itself.
-->
<dict>
    <key>keychain-access-groups</key>
    <array>
        <string>KPUFGR98A5.app.birdo.vpn</string>
    </array>
</dict>
</plist>
